            <fr:title text="Universal Element of a Presheaf">Universal Element of a Presheaf</fr:title>
            <fr:taxon>Definition</fr:taxon>
          </fr:frontmatter>
          <fr:mainmatter>
            <html:p>A <html:em>universal element</html:em> of a presheaf <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[P]]></fr:tex> on a category <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[C]]></fr:tex> is an <fr:link href="/sss-0030/" title="Element of a Presheaf" uri="https://stevenschaefer.net/sss-0030/" display-uri="sss-0030" type="local">element</fr:link> <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[x \in  P c]]></fr:tex>, where <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[c]]></fr:tex> is some object of <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[C]]></fr:tex>, demonstrating that <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[P]]></fr:tex> is representable by <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[c]]></fr:tex>.</html:p>
            <fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[P \cong  よ c]]></fr:tex>
            <html:p>(Slightly) more concretely, a universal element is captured by the following three pieces of data
  <html:ul><html:li>An object <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[c]]></fr:tex> of <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[C]]></fr:tex></html:li>
     <html:li>An element <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[x \in  P c]]></fr:tex></html:li>
     <html:li>A proof that the map sending a morphism <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[f : b \to  c]]></fr:tex> to <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[(Pf)(x)]]></fr:tex> is an equivalence</html:li></html:ul>

  This third point states that morphisms from <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[b]]></fr:tex> into <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[c]]></fr:tex> are uniquely determined by an element of <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[P]]></fr:tex> at the domain <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[b]]></fr:tex>.
</html:p>
            <html:p>Or equivalently, universal elements are terminal in the category of elements.</html:p>
          </fr:mainmatter>
